{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"grusome-ecumenical-polarbear","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# S6 — Bounded concurrency for gate scratch holders\n\n## Why this story exists (added 2026-09-03 on operator input)\n\nOperator: **~10 plan reviews run in parallel frequently**, plus code reviews and autodeploy contending for the same volume.\n\nThis invalidates an assumption the rest of the epic rests on. Option A caps each accumulator via a high-water-mark GC, and **a high-water-mark GC assumes evictable data exists.** Under ~10 concurrent reviews it largely does not.\n\nThe mechanism is documented in the code itself. `src/rebar/_snapshot/janitor.py` relies on POSIX **delete-on-last-close** plus touch-on-read mtime recency, and deliberately takes **no per-reader lease** — a PID+heartbeat lease was spiked and REJECTED as unsound (N readers per entry, PID reuse, crash-stale leases), following Gitaly/Sourcegraph/Bazel/ccache. It also skips entries inside a grace window. So the janitor may UNLINK an in-flight snapshot, but the blocks are not returned until the last reader closes.\n\n**Consequence: during peak concurrency the held bytes are live, not garbage.** A cap set below peak concurrent hold cannot be honoured — the GC runs, finds nothing evictable, and the volume fills anyway. That is the same shape as the failure this epic exists to fix: bug 3276 measured four rounds of prune-based mitigation that structurally could not reach the bytes that mattered (~6.5 GB of orphaned overlay2 invisible to prune, prune's reclaimable ceiling ~1.06 GB against a 29 GB problem).\n\nWithout this story, S2-S5's caps are aspirational under exactly the load that causes the incident.\n\n## Scope\n\nBound how many gate runs may hold scratch concurrently, so a provisioned ceiling is enforceable rather than advisory.\n\nDesign considerations, to be settled in implementation rather than prescribed here:\n- Gate work runs **in-process on a daemon thread** (`src/rebar/_mcp_llm.py` `_spawn_gate_daemon`), so an in-process semaphore bounds MCP-driven gates — but **CLI-driven gates run in separate processes**, so a cross-process bound needs a filesystem or lock-based counter. A bound that only covers one path is not a bound.\n- The limit must be a **config value with a measured default** (operator decision), not a hardcoded constant.\n- Waiting must be bounded and observable: a gate that blocks forever on admission is worse than one that fails fast. Emit a metric for admission waits/rejections so saturation is visible.\n- This bounds MEMORY as well as disk: the same 10x concurrency drives bug 9ea3's OOM (~739 MB measured per complete plan-review x10 = ~7.4 GB on an 8 GiB host carrying 2.17 GB of steady-state containers).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1: a configurable limit bounds how many gate runs may hold scratch at once, defaulting to a value derived from measurement. Verified by a test that starts limit+1 gate runs and asserts the last is admitted only after one releases.\n- [ ] AC2: the bound covers BOTH the in-process MCP daemon path and separate-process CLI gate runs. Verified by a test exercising each path against the same counter.\n- [ ] AC3: admission waiting is bounded and observable — a blocked gate does not wait indefinitely, and waits/rejections publish a metric so saturation is visible. Verified by a test asserting the metric is emitted on a blocked admission.\n- [ ] AC4: the limit is a documented config key with a stated default and rationale, not a hardcoded constant. Verified by reading the config schema and `docs/config.md`.\n- [ ] AC5: releasing is crash-safe — a gate process that dies does not permanently consume a slot. Verified by a test that kills a holder and asserts the slot is reclaimed.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `python -m pytest tests -k \"gate_concurrency or admission\" -q`\n- `make lint && make typecheck`\n- Concurrency shape: this composes locked operations across processes, so route contention cases to the EXISTING shared suites per AGENTS.md rather than a bespoke harness.","id":"09da-343c-1ee9-480c","parent_id":"6202-e1c7-c57f-4897","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"S6 — Bound concurrent gate scratch holders so the caps are enforceable"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788478018887793001,"uuid":"de23ca93-c6de-4cb8-8316-cb4c6e58cd52"}